      <h1>Tankless vs. Traditional Tank Water Heaters: A Practical Comparison</h1>
      <p>
        <time datetime="2025-10-03">October 3, 2025</time>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <article class="prose">
        <p>
          Choosing a new water heater often comes down to two main categories: traditional storage
          tanks and tankless (on-demand) units. Each has strengths depending on household size,
          usage patterns, and installation conditions.
        </p>

        <h2>How Traditional Tank Water Heaters Work</h2>
        <p>
          A tank water heater stores a set volume of hot water—commonly 40 to 50 gallons for many
          homes—and keeps it ready. When you open a hot-water tap, heated water leaves the top of
          the tank while cold water enters the bottom to be heated.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Advantages</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Lower upfront equipment and installation cost in most cases</li>
          <li>Familiar technology with widely available parts</li>
          <li>Can supply multiple fixtures at once within the tank’s capacity</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Considerations</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Standby heat loss as the tank maintains temperature</li>
          <li>Finite hot-water supply during high demand</li>
          <li>Larger physical footprint</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How Tankless Water Heaters Work</h2>
        <p>
          Tankless units heat water only when a tap is opened. Cold water flows through a heat
          exchanger and is warmed on the way to the fixture. There is no storage tank.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Advantages</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Continuous hot water for as long as the unit’s flow rate allows</li>
          <li>Smaller wall-mounted footprint</li>
          <li>Potentially lower operating costs for households that use less hot water overall</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Considerations</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Higher initial purchase and installation cost</li>
          <li>May require gas-line upgrades, new venting, or electrical service changes</li>
          <li>Flow rate limits can affect simultaneous use of multiple fixtures</li>
          <li>Periodic descaling is important, especially with hard water</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Which Option Fits Your Home?</h2>
        <p>
          Households with moderate, staggered hot-water use often do well with a properly sized
          tank. Homes that value endless hot water for back-to-back showers or that have limited
          mechanical-room space may prefer tankless—provided the utility infrastructure can support it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Climate, fuel type (gas vs. electric), and local water hardness also influence the decision.
          A brief on-site evaluation is the most reliable way to match a system to your actual needs.
        </p>

